exams=[
 {'slug':'claude-architect-foundations','level':'Foundations','code':'CCAR-F','questions':60,'summary':'Build your understanding of agent orchestration, tools, Claude Code, structured prompts, and reliable context handling.','domains':[
 ('Agentic Architecture & Orchestration',27,'Define the agent’s goal, available actions, and stopping conditions before adding autonomy. Separate model decisions from deterministic control logic. Treat retries, escalation, and human approval as explicit workflow steps.','Sketch a document-triage agent. Show how it handles missing information, a failed tool call, and a request that requires human approval.'),
 ('Tool Design & MCP Integration',18,'Make each tool’s purpose, inputs, and outputs unambiguous. Validate arguments and enforce permissions in the application. Distinguish tool execution failures from valid empty results so the agent can recover appropriately.','Write a contract for a read-only customer lookup tool, including required inputs, error responses, and the access checks performed before execution.'),
 ('Claude Code Configuration & Workflows',20,'Keep repository guidance specific to the project: build steps, conventions, test expectations, and boundaries. Review proposed changes and use the project’s checks before accepting them. Scope permissions to the work being performed.','Draft repository guidance for a small Python service. Include how to run tests, which files need extra review, and when the assistant should ask for clarification.'),
 ('Prompt Engineering & Structured Output',20,'State the task, constraints, source material, and expected output clearly. Treat retrieved content as data rather than trusted instructions. Validate structured responses against a schema and define what happens when information is missing.','Design a document-extraction prompt and output schema. Include a missing-value policy and test it with incomplete or contradictory input.'),
 ('Context Management & Reliability',15,'Select relevant context and preserve the source of important facts. Long histories need deliberate summarization, while retrieval needs relevance checks. Handle timeouts, malformed outputs, and insufficient evidence with explicit fallback behavior.','Create a recovery plan for an assistant that loses a source reference, receives conflicting documents, or reaches its context limit.') ]},
 {'slug':'claude-architect-professional','level':'Professional','code':'CCAR-P','questions':63,'summary':'Practice architecture trade-offs across enterprise integration, evaluation, governance, and production operations.','domains':[
 ('Solution Design & Architecture',17,'Begin with business outcomes and measurable constraints. Compare a deterministic workflow, a retrieval-assisted application, and an autonomous agent against the same requirements. Make ownership and failure boundaries visible in the design.','Compare two architectures for a service-request assistant using latency, cost, reliability, and approval requirements. Explain which trade-offs you would accept.'),
 ('Claude Models, Prompting & Context Engineering',13,'Choose models and context strategies using representative tasks and measured outcomes. Consider input quality, reasoning demands, latency, and cost together. A larger context window does not remove the need for relevance and evidence tracking.','Define an experiment comparing two model configurations on the same tasks. Specify quality criteria, latency measurements, and a cost budget.'),
 ('Integration',19,'Design interfaces around data ownership, authorization, and failure recovery. Separate read operations from consequential writes. Use appropriate validation, idempotency, and retry policies at service boundaries rather than relying on prompts alone.','Design a RAG assistant that reads internal documents and creates service tickets. Show where retrieval permissions, approval, and duplicate prevention apply.'),
 ('Evaluation, Testing & Optimization',16,'Build a representative evaluation set before optimizing. Break results down by task and failure type, and combine automated checks with human review where judgment matters. Re-test after changes to models, prompts, retrieval, or tools.','Create an evaluation matrix covering answer quality, groundedness, tool selection, latency, and cost. Define the evidence required to release a change.'),
 ('Governance, Safety & Risk Management',14,'Identify sensitive data, risky actions, and accountable owners. Enforce access, retention, and approval policies outside the model. Consider prompt injection and unintended disclosure in the context of actual data flows and permissions.','Threat-model an assistant that processes confidential documents. Define preventive controls, audit events, and escalation paths for the highest-impact failures.'),
 ('Stakeholder Communication & Lifecycle Management',14,'Translate technical results into business consequences and explicit decisions. Agree on acceptance criteria, operating responsibilities, and feedback loops. Plan for model changes, incidents, rollback, and retirement throughout the application lifecycle.','Write a release recommendation that includes measured benefits, unresolved risks, ownership, and rollback criteria for a business sponsor.'),
 ('Developer Productivity & Operational Enablement',7,'Give teams reusable patterns, useful observability, and clear operating guidance. Standardize common practices while preserving project-specific checks. Measure whether the tooling helps teams deliver reliable results rather than only increasing generated output.','Draft an operating checklist covering run traces, tool failures, cost trends, incident response, and how teams share improvements.') ]}
]

# The source guide exposes 30 task statements across the five Foundations domains.
# Keep the task names and source lesson URLs visible so this page works as a study map,
# not just a high-level summary.
foundation_tasks=[
 ('Agentic Architecture & Orchestration',27,'Design and implement agentic systems using Claude’s API, including loop management, orchestration patterns, guardrails, and the Claude Agent SDK.',[
  ('1.1 Agentic Loops','agentic-architecture/1-1-agentic-loops','Use stop_reason as the deterministic loop signal: continue on tool_use and finish on end_turn. Append tool results to the conversation before the next request.'),
  ('1.2 Multi-Agent Orchestration','agentic-architecture/1-2-orchestration-patterns','Use hub-and-spoke coordination for the exam: the coordinator decomposes work, routes context, aggregates results, and keeps subagents from communicating directly.'),
  ('1.3 Subagent Invocation & Context Passing','agentic-architecture/1-3-subagent-invocation-context','Give the coordinator Task/Agent permission, pass context explicitly, and preserve structured metadata such as source URLs and document names.'),
  ('1.4 Workflow Enforcement & Handoff','agentic-architecture/1-4-workflow-enforcement-handoff','Use programmatic gates for money, security, and compliance; prompts are probabilistic, while prerequisite checks and hooks are deterministic.'),
  ('1.5 Agent SDK Hooks','agentic-architecture/1-5-agent-sdk-hooks','Use PreToolUse to block or rewrite a call before execution and PostToolUse to normalise results after execution.'),
  ('1.6 Task Decomposition Strategies','agentic-architecture/1-6-task-decomposition','Match fixed sequential pipelines to predictable work and dynamic decomposition to open-ended investigation; watch for narrow decomposition and attention dilution.'),
  ('1.7 Session State & Resumption','agentic-architecture/1-7-session-state-resumption','Resume when context is still valid, fork for a divergent approach, and start fresh with a structured summary when the old context is stale.') ]),
 ('Tool Design & MCP Integration',18,'Design effective tool schemas, implement MCP servers and clients, and integrate external services into Claude-powered applications.',[
  ('2.1 Tool Interface Design','tool-design-mcp/2-1-tool-schema-design','Descriptions should state purpose, inputs, examples, edge cases, and boundaries. Sharpen descriptions before adding a routing layer.'),
  ('2.2 Structured Error Responses','tool-design-mcp/2-2-structured-error-responses','Return structured errorCategory, isRetryable, and description metadata. Separate access failures from valid empty results; retry transient failures, not business or permission failures.'),
  ('2.3 Tool Distribution & Tool Choice','tool-design-mcp/2-3-tool-distribution-choice','Keep agents near four or five role-specific tools, consolidate same-shaped operations, and use auto, any, or forced tool selection for the right guarantee.'),
  ('2.4 MCP Server Integration','tool-design-mcp/2-4-mcp-server-integration','Use project .mcp.json for shared configuration, user-level settings for personal connections, environment variables for secrets, and community servers for standard integrations.'),
  ('2.5 Built-in Tools','tool-design-mcp/2-5-built-in-tools','Remember the Claude Code tools: Read, Write, Edit, Bash, Grep, and Glob. Grep searches file contents; Glob matches file paths.') ]),
 ('Claude Code Configuration & Workflows',20,'Configure Claude Code for development workflows, manage settings, hooks, permissions, and integrate with CI/CD pipelines.',[
  ('3.1 CLAUDE.md Hierarchy, Scoping & Modular Organisation','claude-code-config/3-1-claude-md-hierarchy','Project instructions are shared through git; user instructions are personal. CLAUDE.md files are guidance, not deterministic enforcement.'),
  ('3.2 Custom Slash Commands & Skills','claude-code-config/3-2-slash-commands-skills','Use project .claude/skills or .claude/commands for shared workflows; user-level folders are personal. Skills are directories containing SKILL.md.'),
  ('3.3 Path-Specific Rules','claude-code-config/3-3-path-specific-rules','Use path-scoped rules for conventions that should load only for matching files; this keeps irrelevant context out of a session.'),
  ('3.4 Plan Mode vs Direct Execution','claude-code-config/3-4-plan-mode-execution','Use direct execution for clear, limited changes and plan mode for ambiguous work spanning decisions or modules.'),
  ('3.5 Iterative Refinement Techniques','claude-code-config/3-5-iterative-refinement','Use concrete examples when the desired transformation is known, interview first in unfamiliar domains, and batch feedback when changes interact.'),
  ('3.6 CI/CD Integration','claude-code-config/3-6-cicd-integration','Use -p/--print for non-interactive CI runs and machine-readable output options when downstream automation consumes the result.') ]),
 ('Prompt Engineering & Structured Output',20,'Craft effective prompts, implement structured output patterns, and apply production prompt-engineering techniques.',[
  ('4.1 System Prompts with Explicit Criteria','prompt-engineering/4-1-system-prompts','Replace vague confidence language with explicit categories, boundaries, and concrete examples; temporarily disable high-false-positive categories while refining them.'),
  ('4.2 Few-Shot Prompting','prompt-engineering/4-2-few-shot-prompting','Use representative input/output examples when detailed instructions still produce inconsistent formats, judgements, or extraction fields.'),
  ('4.3 Structured Output with Tool Use','prompt-engineering/4-3-structured-output','Schemas prevent syntax errors but not semantic errors. Use nullable fields for genuinely missing data and tool_choice any when a tool call is required.'),
  ('4.4 Validation, Retry & Feedback Loops','prompt-engineering/4-4-validation-retry-loops','Retry with the original source, failed output, and specific validation error; do not retry when the source cannot contain the missing information.'),
  ('4.5 Batch Processing Strategies','prompt-engineering/4-5-batch-processing','Refine prompts on a representative sample before batch submission. Batch is cost-efficient but latency-tolerant; tool loops remain synchronous.'),
  ('4.6 Multi-Instance & Multi-Pass Review','prompt-engineering/4-6-multi-pass-review','Use independent review instances, per-file passes, integration review, and calibrated confidence routing instead of trusting the generator to review itself.') ]),
 ('Context Management & Reliability',15,'Manage context windows effectively, implement caching strategies, handle long conversations, and build reliable production systems.',[
  ('5.1 Context Window Management','context-management/5-1-context-window-management','Protect transactional facts in a persistent case-facts block, put key findings first, trim tool results, and place stable prefixes before cache breakpoints.'),
  ('5.2 Escalation & Ambiguity Resolution','context-management/5-2-escalation-ambiguity','Escalate on an explicit human request, a policy gap, or inability to progress; do not use sentiment or raw self-reported confidence as the trigger.'),
  ('5.3 Error Propagation in Multi-Agent Systems','context-management/5-3-error-propagation','Recover transient failures locally, propagate structured failure context and partial results, and never hide a timeout as an empty success.'),
  ('5.4 Codebase Exploration & Context Degradation','context-management/5-4-codebase-exploration','Explore incrementally, delegate verbose discovery to isolate context, and persist key findings in scratchpads or crash-recovery manifests.'),
  ('5.5 Human Review & Confidence Calibration','context-management/5-5-human-review-calibration','Measure accuracy by document type and field segment, calibrate confidence on labelled data, and use stratified sampling before reducing human review.'),
  ('5.6 Information Provenance & Multi-Source Synthesis','context-management/5-6-information-provenance','Preserve claim, source URL, document, excerpt, and date. Annotate conflicts instead of selecting a value arbitrarily.') ])]
